489 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 405 0 R
/E (The 2025 Charlotte Music Census was a community-led initiative designed to better understand the strengths and challenges of the local music ecosystem. The Charlotte Music Census launched on October 22, 2025, and ran through November 28, 2025 and collected 1,046 responses from across the creative, industry, and venue/presenter sectors.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
490 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 405 0 R
/E (Charlotte music ecosystem respondents are predominantly Music Creatives \(70%\), but healthy representation from Industry \(20%\) and Venue/Presenter roles \(10%\). Respondents report high levels of experience \(over 10 years in music\), reflecting a mature music community.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
491 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /BlockQuote
/P 405 0 R
/T (Creative respondents represent a wide range of roles, including performers, songwriters, educators, producers, and engineers. Top career concerns include rising cost of living and pay rates not keeping pace with expenses, alongside challenges related to sustaining consistent music work. Creative respondents report significant annual spending on their music practice, contributing a collective $4.7 million in total creative spending each year, with 44% of that staying local. )
/E (Creative respondents represent a wide range of roles, including performers, songwriters, educators, producers, and engineers. Top career concerns include rising cost of living and pay rates not keeping pace with expenses, alongside challenges related to sustaining consistent music work. Creative respondents report significant annual spending on their music practice, contributing a collective $4.7 million in total creative spending each year, with 44% of that staying local. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
492 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 405 0 R
/E (Industry respondents reflect a diverse set of occupations, led by event and venue workers, music marketing professionals, and music development roles. Venue and Presenter respondents represent a mix of ownership types and facility sizes, with many operating small- to mid-sized spaces. On average, Charlotte venues present 93 live events per venue per year, totaling roughly 5,108 live events annually. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
493 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 405 0 R
/E (Venues and presenters report navigating a complex regulatory environment, with sound policy, permitting processes, and regulatory confusion ranking among the most impactful challenges. Respondents express strong interest in tools that improve clarity and coordination, including a centralized live music regulations webpage, a single point of contact, and tiered compliance approaches. When asked about financial assistance, venue and presenter respondents most often prefer microgrants and tax-based incentives.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
494 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /BlockQuote
/P 405 0 R
/T (Music-related income represents a meaningful but limited share of overall earnings for many respondents. Across all respondents, music activity generates an estimated $31 million in annual income, with respondents reporting that an average of 30% of their total annual income comes from music-related work. Average household income among respondents is $99,000, while average annual income derived specifically from music is $29,000. A majority work outside of music to supplement income, and median pay for performances remains low. These dynamics highlight the importance of workforce development, income stability, and clearer career pathways. Respondents most frequently identify marketing \(53%\), audio and video recording or engineering \(50%\), and booking, promotion, and event production \(49%\) as priority skill areas, pointing to demand for practical, career-sustaining workforce support.)
/E (Music-related income represents a meaningful but limited share of overall earnings for many respondents. Across all respondents, music activity generates an estimated $31 million in annual income, with respondents reporting that an average of 30% of their total annual income comes from music-related work. Average household income among respondents is $99,000, while average annual income derived specifically from music is $29,000. A majority work outside of music to supplement income, and median pay for performances remains low. These dynamics highlight the importance of workforce development, income stability, and clearer career pathways. Respondents most frequently identify marketing \(53%\), audio and video recording or engineering \(50%\), and booking, promotion, and event production \(49%\) as priority skill areas, pointing to demand for practical, career-sustaining workforce support.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
495 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 405 0 R
/E (Charlotte respondents identify connection and collaboration as priorities for strengthening the music ecosystem. Preferences include better access to music services, opportunities to collaborate, stronger audience development, and greater integration of local music into public spaces and community life.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
496 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 405 0 R
/E (The Charlotte music ecosystem is widely viewed as safe, inclusive, and welcoming, but respondents also describe underlying tension around elitism and ageism. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
