/T (Cities Traveled to for Music-Related Work - Respondents reported traveling primarily within the Southeast for music-related work. The most commonly cited destinations include the Triangle region of North Carolina \(41%\), the Triad \(38%\), and Asheville, NC \(32%\). Nearly one-third of respondents \(32%\) reported not traveling for music-related work in the past year. Other frequently mentioned destinations include Charleston, SC \(24%\), Atlanta, GA \(23%\), Greenville, SC \(22%\), Nashville, TN \(16%\), Washington, DC \(13%\), and Richmond, VA \(13%\).)
/E (Cities Traveled to for Music-Related Work - Respondents reported traveling primarily within the Southeast for music-related work. The most commonly cited destinations include the Triangle region of North Carolina \(41%\), the Triad \(38%\), and Asheville, NC \(32%\). Nearly one-third of respondents \(32%\) reported not traveling for music-related work in the past year. Other frequently mentioned destinations include Charleston, SC \(24%\), Atlanta, GA \(23%\), Greenville, SC \(22%\), Nashville, TN \(16%\), Washington, DC \(13%\), and Richmond, VA \(13%\).)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
580 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 1834 0 R
/T (Genre Identification - Respondents identify with a wide range of musical genres. The most commonly selected genres include Rock \(19%\) and Pop/Singer-Songwriter \(18%\), followed by Jazz/Blues \(13%\), R&B/Soul/Funk/Neo-Soul \(12%\), and Country/Americana/Folk/Bluegrass \(11%\). Classical/Symphony/Chamber/Opera represents 9% of respondents, while Hip-Hop/Rap/Trap \(6%\), Gospel/Contemporary Christian \(5%\), Electronic/Dance/DJ/House \(4%\), and Latin/Caribbean/World \(3%\) reflect additional stylistic diversity within the ecosystem.)
/E (Genre Identification - Respondents identify with a wide range of musical genres. The most commonly selected genres include Rock \(19%\) and Pop/Singer-Songwriter \(18%\), followed by Jazz/Blues \(13%\), R&B/Soul/Funk/Neo-Soul \(12%\), and Country/Americana/Folk/Bluegrass \(11%\). Classical/Symphony/Chamber/Opera represents 9% of respondents, while Hip-Hop/Rap/Trap \(6%\), Gospel/Contemporary Christian \(5%\), Electronic/Dance/DJ/House \(4%\), and Latin/Caribbean/World \(3%\) reflect additional stylistic diversity within the ecosystem.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [7]
