/T (income to support music careers. The data reflects a predominantly freelance and independent workforce, with many respondents working outside of music for income stability, benefits, and flexibility. Local performance remains a primary source of earned income with an average pay of $505/performance, and touring activity is limited for many respondents, contributing to a median touring pay of $200. Creative respondents continue to reinvest significantly in the local music economy, spending an estimated $4.7 million collectively each year on music-related expenses, with 44% of that spending occurring locally.)
/E (income to support music careers. The data reflects a predominantly freelance and independent workforce, with many respondents working outside of music for income stability, benefits, and flexibility. Local performance remains a primary source of earned income with an average pay of $505/performance, and touring activity is limited for many respondents, contributing to a median touring pay of $200. Creative respondents continue to reinvest significantly in the local music economy, spending an estimated $4.7 million collectively each year on music-related expenses, with 44% of that spending occurring locally.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
762 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 1939 0 R
/T (Music Job Type/Compensation - Respondents primarily report working as independent or freelance music workers. Over half \(57%\) indicate earning income through independent or direct payment, while 27% report earning wages from an employer. An additional 16% report being unpaid for their music work, reflecting the continued prevalence of uncompensated labor within the local music ecosystem. The majority of respondents identify as freelancers or sole proprietors \(57%\), with others working as part of bands \(30%\) or registered businesses \(29%\), underscoring the entrepreneurial nature of music work.)
/E (Music Job Type/Compensation - Respondents primarily report working as independent or freelance music workers. Over half \(57%\) indicate earning income through independent or direct payment, while 27% report earning wages from an employer. An additional 16% report being unpaid for their music work, reflecting the continued prevalence of uncompensated labor within the local music ecosystem. The majority of respondents identify as freelancers or sole proprietors \(57%\), with others working as part of bands \(30%\) or registered businesses \(29%\), underscoring the entrepreneurial nature of music work.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
