/T (Venue Live Events and Local Talent Bookings - Venue respondents report an average of 93 live music events per venue annually, totaling more than 5,100 live events across respondents. Local artists are regularly included in programming, with 62% of presenters reporting booking local talent as headliners for all or most events, and 71% reporting the same for support slots. These responses indicate that venues and presenters play a critical role in sustaining opportunities for local artists, even as overall performance volume and pay vary widely across the ecosystem. )
/E (Venue Live Events and Local Talent Bookings - Venue respondents report an average of 93 live music events per venue annually, totaling more than 5,100 live events across respondents. Local artists are regularly included in programming, with 62% of presenters reporting booking local talent as headliners for all or most events, and 71% reporting the same for support slots. These responses indicate that venues and presenters play a critical role in sustaining opportunities for local artists, even as overall performance volume and pay vary widely across the ecosystem. )
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
798 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 1957 0 R
/T (Creative Performance Frequency & Pay -  Local paid performance activity varies widely among respondents. While 50% report one or more paid local performances per month, nearly one-quarter \(23%\) report no paid local performances in a typical month. Touring activity is more limited, with 56% reporting no paid touring performances in the most recent month. As a result, while average touring pay exceeds $300 per performance, the median touring pay is $200, reflecting that more than half of respondents did not tour during the period reported. This reinforces the uneven and episodic nature of touring income for many Creatives.)
/E (Creative Performance Frequency & Pay -  Local paid performance activity varies widely among respondents. While 50% report one or more paid local performances per month, nearly one-quarter \(23%\) report no paid local performances in a typical month. Touring activity is more limited, with 56% reporting no paid touring performances in the most recent month. As a result, while average touring pay exceeds $300 per performance, the median touring pay is $200, reflecting that more than half of respondents did not tour during the period reported. This reinforces the uneven and episodic nature of touring income for many Creatives.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [17]
